1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,580
This is Doing and
Feeling, the long version.

2
00:00:03,180 --> 00:00:04,820
Episode ten: The Evidence.

3
00:00:05,160 --> 00:00:10,910
In 2008, a team led by the neuroscientist
Beatrice de Gelder reported a remarkable

4
00:00:10,960 --> 00:00:12,740
experiment, and filmed it.

5
00:00:12,980 --> 00:00:15,220
Their subject, known as T.

6
00:00:15,270 --> 00:00:19,590
N., was a physician who had suffered two
strokes that destroyed the primary visual

7
00:00:19,640 --> 00:00:21,680
cortex on both sides of his brain.

8
00:00:21,820 --> 00:00:24,020
By every clinical test he was blind.

9
00:00:24,280 --> 00:00:27,120
He walked with a cane and
said he saw nothing at all.

10
00:00:27,620 --> 00:00:32,430
The researchers set up a long corridor
cluttered with boxes and a chair, took away

11
00:00:32,480 --> 00:00:34,720
his cane, and asked him to walk down it.

12
00:00:34,900 --> 00:00:39,890
He did, weaving carefully around every
obstacle, while insisting he could not see

13
00:00:39,940 --> 00:00:44,790
them. That is the split between doing and
feeling from episode one, in a hospital

14
00:00:44,840 --> 00:00:49,550
corridor. Something in his brain was
processing visual information well enough to

15
00:00:49,600 --> 00:00:53,660
steer his body. Yet, as far as he
could tell, none of it was seen.

16
00:00:54,200 --> 00:00:58,620
Two episodes ago we met a novel that
imagined a whole species built that way.

17
00:00:58,880 --> 00:01:01,020
Here was one man, for one sense.

18
00:01:01,280 --> 00:01:03,060
This episode is about evidence:

19
00:01:03,220 --> 00:01:07,020
what the science of consciousness can
actually measure, and how far to trust it.

20
00:01:07,300 --> 00:01:11,830
The methods, the patients, the hidden
awareness of people who seem unresponsive, the

21
00:01:11,880 --> 00:01:15,790
closest thing we have to a consciousness
meter, the states that switch experience

22
00:01:15,840 --> 00:01:17,280
off and on, and the animals.

23
00:01:17,780 --> 00:01:21,890
Along the way, we'll find that some of the
field's most famous results are shakier

24
00:01:21,940 --> 00:01:25,420
than their reputations. Theories
wait until next episode.

25
00:01:25,680 --> 00:01:32,350
Today is data. The modern program began
in 1990, when Francis Crick and Christof

26
00:01:32,400 --> 00:01:37,230
Koch proposed setting the philosophical
puzzle aside for a while and hunting for the

27
00:01:37,280 --> 00:01:38,840
neural correlates of consciousness.

28
00:01:39,400 --> 00:01:42,340
David Chalmers later gave
the careful definition:

29
00:01:42,680 --> 00:01:47,370
a neural correlate of a conscious state
is a minimal system whose activity is

30
00:01:47,420 --> 00:01:51,160
sufficient, under the right
background conditions, for that state.

31
00:01:51,340 --> 00:01:55,220
The core method is what Bernard
Baars called contrastive analysis.

32
00:01:55,600 --> 00:02:00,390
Hold the stimulus constant, arrange for
it to be consciously perceived on some

33
00:02:00,440 --> 00:02:04,480
trials and not on others, and compare
brain activity between the two.

34
00:02:04,740 --> 00:02:07,420
Whatever differs is a candidate correlate.

35
00:02:07,700 --> 00:02:12,140
But there's a trap, and much of the last
fifteen years has been about escaping it.

36
00:02:12,440 --> 00:02:16,850
Whatever differs between seen and
unseen trials includes far more than the

37
00:02:16,900 --> 00:02:22,550
experience itself. It includes prerequisites,
like attention, expectation, and

38
00:02:22,600 --> 00:02:25,750
arousal, which must be in place
before you can see anything.

39
00:02:25,800 --> 00:02:27,660
And it includes consequences:

40
00:02:28,080 --> 00:02:32,260
the act of reporting, the memory of
having seen, your confidence about it.

41
00:02:32,440 --> 00:02:38,590
In 2012, Jaan Aru and colleagues, and
separately Tom de Graaf and colleagues, made

42
00:02:38,640 --> 00:02:42,990
this argument forcefully. A naive
contrast picks up the machinery around

43
00:02:43,040 --> 00:02:45,380
consciousness along with
consciousness itself.

44
00:02:45,760 --> 00:02:48,760
Every machine learning
person knows that problem.

45
00:02:48,980 --> 00:02:53,610
If your probe can't separate a property
from its correlates, you measure the

46
00:02:53,660 --> 00:02:55,280
correlates and declare victory.

47
00:02:55,780 --> 00:03:00,000
Exactly. And the field's response has
been to invent cleverer contrasts.

48
00:03:01,060 --> 00:03:05,800
Here is the toolkit, because these methods
are the experimental heart of the field.

49
00:03:06,380 --> 00:03:10,930
Masking: flash an image for a few hundredths
of a second, then replace it with a

50
00:03:10,980 --> 00:03:16,410
pattern. Set the timing at threshold, and
the very same flash is seen on some trials

51
00:03:16,460 --> 00:03:19,200
and missed on others. Binocular rivalry:

52
00:03:19,520 --> 00:03:23,700
show each eye a different image, say a
face to one and a house to the other.

53
00:03:24,100 --> 00:03:28,810
Instead of a blend, your experience
alternates every few seconds, while the input

54
00:03:28,860 --> 00:03:34,650
never changes. Nikos Logothetis and his
colleagues trained monkeys to report which

55
00:03:34,700 --> 00:03:38,830
image they were seeing during rivalry,
and recorded neurons along the visual

56
00:03:38,880 --> 00:03:43,770
pathway. In the earliest visual areas,
only a small minority of cells followed the

57
00:03:43,820 --> 00:03:48,040
reported percept. In an intermediate
area, a bit more than a third did.

58
00:03:48,320 --> 00:03:53,080
In the high-level face and object areas of
the temporal lobe, about nine in ten did.

59
00:03:53,440 --> 00:03:58,270
In those single neurons, awareness seemed
to be reflected late in the hierarchy, not

60
00:03:58,320 --> 00:04:03,590
early. Later brain imaging in humans,
though, found percept-linked signals even in

61
00:04:03,640 --> 00:04:09,610
primary visual cortex. Continuous flash
suppression, introduced by Naotsugu Tsuchiya

62
00:04:09,660 --> 00:04:14,040
and Koch in 2005, is rivalry
turned up to full strength.

63
00:04:14,600 --> 00:04:19,570
Flash rapidly changing colorful patterns
to one eye, and a static picture shown to

64
00:04:19,620 --> 00:04:24,090
the other eye can stay invisible for many
seconds, long enough to test what the

65
00:04:24,140 --> 00:04:25,820
brain does with things you never see.

66
00:04:26,380 --> 00:04:31,660
Inattentional blindness: in Daniel Simons
and Christopher Chabris's famous video,

67
00:04:32,080 --> 00:04:37,050
people counting basketball passes often
fail to notice a person in a gorilla suit

68
00:04:37,100 --> 00:04:38,480
strolling through the scene.

69
00:04:38,760 --> 00:04:42,550
Then one of the most important
methodological innovations of recent years:

70
00:04:42,600 --> 00:04:48,090
no-report paradigms. If report contaminates
the contrast, infer what someone is

71
00:04:48,140 --> 00:04:49,600
experiencing without asking.

72
00:04:50,020 --> 00:04:54,410
During rivalry, the eyes make involuntary
tracking movements that follow whichever

73
00:04:54,460 --> 00:05:00,640
image is dominant. In 2014, Stefan Frässle
and colleagues used those movements,

74
00:05:00,820 --> 00:05:04,880
along with pupil size, to read out
the percept with no report at all.

75
00:05:05,120 --> 00:05:10,110
Frontal activity linked to rivalry was
reduced when report was removed, but not

76
00:05:10,160 --> 00:05:13,130
abolished, and some frontal
regions stayed active.

77
00:05:13,180 --> 00:05:19,350
Removing report also changes how people
attend, so even this cleaner contrast is not

78
00:05:19,400 --> 00:05:24,390
perfectly clean. That result is ammunition
for both sides of the front-versus-back

79
00:05:24,440 --> 00:05:31,010
debate next episode. And in October 2026,
Doris Tsao's lab, which had already used a

80
00:05:31,060 --> 00:05:35,990
no-report design with single face cells,
posted a preprint based on recordings from

81
00:05:36,040 --> 00:05:37,640
thousands of neurons at once.

82
00:05:37,900 --> 00:05:42,910
Two monkeys passively watched displays
in which one face was flashed and another

83
00:05:42,960 --> 00:05:47,370
suppressed. In face-selective regions,
activity flipped back and forth between

84
00:05:47,420 --> 00:05:51,770
representing the seen face and the suppressed
one, each state lasting roughly sixty

85
00:05:51,820 --> 00:05:57,070
milliseconds. It's two animals, from one
lab, not yet peer reviewed, and the face

86
00:05:57,120 --> 00:05:59,740
counted as seen was the
one human viewers reported.

87
00:06:00,040 --> 00:06:03,110
A switching rate is not a
frame rate for experience.

88
00:06:03,160 --> 00:06:08,090
But it hints that what you see may be a
rapidly contested state, rather than a

89
00:06:08,140 --> 00:06:13,090
steady one. Now a measurement problem
that sounds technical, and isn't.

90
00:06:13,140 --> 00:06:15,260
How do you know someone
didn't see something?

91
00:06:15,520 --> 00:06:17,980
The obvious answer is that they told you.

92
00:06:18,200 --> 00:06:20,340
But a report depends on two things:

93
00:06:20,560 --> 00:06:24,860
how much information gets through, and
how willing a person is to say yes.

94
00:06:25,320 --> 00:06:29,760
Signal detection theory, borrowed from
radar engineering, separates them.

95
00:06:30,140 --> 00:06:33,140
Sensitivity is how well you
can tell signal from noise.

96
00:06:33,720 --> 00:06:37,960
Criterion is how much evidence you
demand before saying, I saw it.

97
00:06:38,960 --> 00:06:43,010
A cautious observer with a strict criterion
will say nothing was there on many

98
00:06:43,060 --> 00:06:45,660
trials where some faint
experience was present.

99
00:06:45,940 --> 00:06:52,210
This is the criterion problem, and in 2021
the philosopher Ian Phillips used it to

100
00:06:52,260 --> 00:06:54,360
challenge one of the
field's founding cases.

101
00:06:55,100 --> 00:06:59,120
Blindsight, he argued, may not be
vision without experience at all.

102
00:06:59,320 --> 00:07:04,430
It may be degraded conscious vision, so
faint and strange that patients, applying

103
00:07:04,480 --> 00:07:08,040
the standard of normal sight,
sincerely report seeing nothing.

104
00:07:08,960 --> 00:07:13,950
On that reading, blindsight shows how
unreliable a report of nothing can be, rather

105
00:07:14,000 --> 00:07:17,260
than showing that sophisticated
vision can run in the dark.

106
00:07:17,540 --> 00:07:22,450
So the most famous evidence for doing
without feeling might be feeling that's too

107
00:07:22,500 --> 00:07:27,650
weak to count? That's the live question,
and worth holding in mind for every patient

108
00:07:27,700 --> 00:07:32,650
case in this episode. Defenders of the
classic reading point to signal detection

109
00:07:32,700 --> 00:07:36,950
studies suggesting that blindsight
differs from faint normal vision.

110
00:07:37,000 --> 00:07:42,130
The debate is unresolved, but it has
pushed the field to measure confidence and

111
00:07:42,180 --> 00:07:45,460
response bias far more
routinely than it once did.

112
00:07:46,420 --> 00:07:48,680
With that caution in hand, the patients.

113
00:07:49,200 --> 00:07:52,960
Blindsight was named by Lawrence
Weiskrantz, whose patient D.

114
00:07:53,010 --> 00:07:56,790
B. had part of his primary
visual cortex surgically removed.

115
00:07:56,840 --> 00:08:02,050
D. B. denied seeing anything in the
affected half of his visual field, yet when

116
00:08:02,100 --> 00:08:06,850
forced to guess, he could point to objects
there and discriminate orientation and

117
00:08:06,900 --> 00:08:08,430
movement far above chance.

118
00:08:08,480 --> 00:08:15,020
Weiskrantz and colleagues' key report
appeared in 1974, and his monograph in 1986.

119
00:08:15,660 --> 00:08:20,830
Some blindsight patients report no experience
at all, others a vague feeling that

120
00:08:20,880 --> 00:08:23,800
something is there. And
there's affective blindsight:

121
00:08:24,180 --> 00:08:29,490
patients respond, above chance, to the
emotional expressions of faces they say they

122
00:08:29,540 --> 00:08:33,400
cannot see. Hemispatial neglect
is in some ways stranger.

123
00:08:33,780 --> 00:08:38,910
After damage typically to the right parietal
lobe, patients ignore the left side of

124
00:08:38,960 --> 00:08:45,650
space. In 1978, Edoardo Bisiach and
Claudio Luzzatti asked neglect patients

125
00:08:45,700 --> 00:08:50,260
in Milan to imagine standing in the
cathedral square and describe what they saw.

126
00:08:50,420 --> 00:08:53,440
They described only the buildings
on their imagined right.

127
00:08:53,980 --> 00:08:58,250
Asked to imagine standing at the opposite
end of the square, they described the

128
00:08:58,300 --> 00:09:01,300
other side, and omitted what
they had just described.

129
00:09:01,560 --> 00:09:04,960
The neglect lived in the
representation of space itself.

130
00:09:05,340 --> 00:09:07,860
Then there's the most
famous neglect case of all.

131
00:09:08,160 --> 00:09:13,630
In 1988, John Marshall and Peter Halligan
reported a patient shown two drawings of a

132
00:09:13,680 --> 00:09:17,880
house, identical except that one had
flames coming from its left side.

133
00:09:18,140 --> 00:09:20,040
She said the drawings were the same.

134
00:09:20,460 --> 00:09:24,690
Yet when asked which house she would rather
live in, she mostly chose the one that

135
00:09:24,740 --> 00:09:29,570
wasn't burning. It became a textbook
example of unconscious information guiding

136
00:09:29,620 --> 00:09:32,740
choice. Here's what the
textbooks often leave out.

137
00:09:32,900 --> 00:09:39,230
It was a single patient. A 1990 follow-up
by Bisiach and Maria Luisa Rusconi got

138
00:09:39,280 --> 00:09:45,190
inconsistent results. And in 2025, a
preregistered replication tested fifty-four

139
00:09:45,240 --> 00:09:48,040
stroke patients, twenty-one
of them with neglect.

140
00:09:48,380 --> 00:09:52,600
The burning-house effect appeared in just
one patient, who did not have neglect.

141
00:09:53,380 --> 00:09:58,010
Exploratory analyses suggested that
when people did show a preference, they

142
00:09:58,060 --> 00:10:02,550
preferred intact drawings over merely
shaded ones, so the effect may not have been

143
00:10:02,600 --> 00:10:07,660
about fire at all. One patient, and
possibly about shading, not fire.

144
00:10:07,960 --> 00:10:10,720
How many textbook cases
rest on one patient?

145
00:10:11,160 --> 00:10:15,910
More than you'd like. Others have held
up well, like the imagined piazza.

146
00:10:15,960 --> 00:10:20,760
The lesson is to weigh a famous single
case by how often it has been reproduced,

147
00:10:20,940 --> 00:10:23,600
rather than by how often
it has been retold.

148
00:10:23,880 --> 00:10:25,480
Then the split-brain patients.

149
00:10:25,860 --> 00:10:30,830
To treat severe epilepsy, some patients
had the corpus callosum, the main bridge

150
00:10:30,880 --> 00:10:32,980
between the hemispheres, surgically cut.

151
00:10:33,440 --> 00:10:38,470
Roger Sperry, who shared a Nobel Prize for
this work, and Michael Gazzaniga showed

152
00:10:38,520 --> 00:10:41,840
that each hemisphere could respond
to information the other didn't have.

153
00:10:42,200 --> 00:10:46,950
In Gazzaniga's most famous demonstration,
the speaking left hemisphere saw a chicken

154
00:10:47,000 --> 00:10:50,060
claw, and the right
hemisphere saw a snowy scene.

155
00:10:50,200 --> 00:10:54,370
The right hand picked a picture of a
chicken, and the left hand, controlled by the

156
00:10:54,420 --> 00:10:55,940
right hemisphere, picked a shovel.

157
00:10:56,420 --> 00:11:00,630
Asked why, the left hemisphere, which
never saw the snow, answered without

158
00:11:00,680 --> 00:11:05,030
hesitation: the claw goes with the chicken,
and you need a shovel to clean out the

159
00:11:05,080 --> 00:11:09,520
chicken shed. Gazzaniga called the system
behind such answers the interpreter:

160
00:11:09,860 --> 00:11:15,010
a process that generates fluent, confident
explanations for behavior whose real

161
00:11:15,060 --> 00:11:20,290
causes it cannot see. For anyone who works
with language models, that's the most

162
00:11:20,340 --> 00:11:22,080
important result in this episode.

163
00:11:22,180 --> 00:11:28,120
An articulate explanation of a system's
own behavior tells you nothing, on its own,

164
00:11:28,500 --> 00:11:31,420
about whether the explanation
is causally accurate.

165
00:11:31,760 --> 00:11:37,130
That's the parallel drawn in episode six,
where a model described carrying digits it

166
00:11:37,180 --> 00:11:41,010
never carried, and we'll hold onto
it for episodes twelve and thirteen.

167
00:11:41,060 --> 00:11:43,630
And it isn't confined to split brains.

168
00:11:43,680 --> 00:11:48,310
In choice-blindness experiments, the
next entry in episode eight's file on

169
00:11:48,360 --> 00:11:53,230
introspection, Petter Johansson and Lars
Hall had people choose which of two faces

170
00:11:53,280 --> 00:11:57,870
they found more attractive, then secretly
handed them the other photo and asked them

171
00:11:57,920 --> 00:12:03,290
to explain their choice. On most of the
manipulated trials, people didn't notice the

172
00:12:03,340 --> 00:12:07,440
swap, and explained why they had preferred
a face they had actually rejected.

173
00:12:07,740 --> 00:12:11,020
Even the split brain itself
is less settled than it looks.

174
00:12:11,460 --> 00:12:16,690
In 2017, Yair Pinto and colleagues
reported two patients who could respond to

175
00:12:16,740 --> 00:12:20,910
stimuli anywhere in the visual field with
either hand, though they could not compare

176
00:12:20,960 --> 00:12:25,530
stimuli across the two halves, and suggested
that perception may be split while the

177
00:12:25,580 --> 00:12:27,580
person remains one conscious agent.

178
00:12:27,860 --> 00:12:30,560
With two patients, that is contested.

179
00:12:31,360 --> 00:12:36,580
The most consequential findings concern
patients who seem completely unresponsive.

180
00:12:36,920 --> 00:12:38,380
Clinically, there's a spectrum.

181
00:12:38,860 --> 00:12:41,300
In coma, patients cannot be roused.

182
00:12:41,520 --> 00:12:46,550
In the vegetative state, now often called
unresponsive wakefulness syndrome, they

183
00:12:46,600 --> 00:12:50,660
have sleep-wake cycles and open their
eyes, but show no sign of awareness.

184
00:12:50,960 --> 00:12:55,780
In the minimally conscious state, signs
of awareness are inconsistent but genuine.

185
00:12:56,520 --> 00:12:58,400
Bedside diagnosis is hard.

186
00:12:58,660 --> 00:13:04,170
In a well-known 2009 study using a
careful standardized scale, eighteen of

187
00:13:04,220 --> 00:13:09,690
forty-four patients diagnosed as vegetative
by clinical consensus, about forty-one

188
00:13:09,740 --> 00:13:12,180
percent, turned out to
be minimally conscious.

189
00:13:12,720 --> 00:13:18,110
In 2006, Adrian Owen and colleagues published
a short paper in Science that changed

190
00:13:18,160 --> 00:13:23,910
the field. A twenty-three-year-old woman,
five months after a traffic accident, met

191
00:13:23,960 --> 00:13:26,300
every criterion for the vegetative state.

192
00:13:26,580 --> 00:13:32,530
In an fMRI scanner, she was asked to
imagine playing tennis, and then to imagine

193
00:13:32,580 --> 00:13:34,340
walking through the rooms of her house.

194
00:13:34,520 --> 00:13:37,200
Her brain activity matched
that of healthy volunteers:

195
00:13:37,780 --> 00:13:41,780
motor planning areas for tennis,
spatial navigation areas for the house.

196
00:13:42,060 --> 00:13:43,720
She was following instructions.

197
00:13:44,120 --> 00:13:48,070
It was a single patient, and critics
wondered whether the words alone triggered

198
00:13:48,120 --> 00:13:53,350
automatic responses. But in 2010, a
larger study found that five of fifty-four

199
00:13:53,400 --> 00:13:57,350
patients could modulate their brain
activity this way, and one could answer

200
00:13:57,400 --> 00:14:01,500
yes-or-no questions, imagining
tennis for yes and the house for no.

201
00:14:01,980 --> 00:14:07,510
Then, in 2024, a study in the New England
Journal of Medicine led by Yelena Bodien

202
00:14:07,560 --> 00:14:12,270
tested three hundred and fifty-three
patients at six centers in Europe and North

203
00:14:12,320 --> 00:14:16,570
America. Of the two hundred and forty-one
who could not follow commands at the

204
00:14:16,620 --> 00:14:23,670
bedside, sixty, a quarter, showed covert
responses on brain imaging or E.E.G.

205
00:14:23,720 --> 00:14:26,660
The field calls this
cognitive motor dissociation.

206
00:14:26,820 --> 00:14:31,410
And among patients who could follow commands
at the bedside, only about thirty-eight

207
00:14:31,460 --> 00:14:34,180
percent showed responses on the scans.

208
00:14:34,230 --> 00:14:39,530
The tests miss many people who are plainly
aware, so a negative scan cannot rule

209
00:14:39,580 --> 00:14:42,400
awareness out. A quarter, in this group.

210
00:14:42,620 --> 00:14:46,780
But following commands in a scanner
is still access, in Block's sense.

211
00:14:47,060 --> 00:14:49,360
Does it tell us anything about experience?

212
00:14:49,740 --> 00:14:55,450
In a person, access is the best evidence
of experience we ever get, and we grant it

213
00:14:55,500 --> 00:14:56,840
to each other on far less.

214
00:14:56,980 --> 00:15:02,790
So it raises urgent questions about pain,
communication, and decisions to withdraw

215
00:15:02,840 --> 00:15:07,790
care. And it's the clearest demonstration
that behavior can radically underestimate

216
00:15:07,840 --> 00:15:13,570
consciousness. Can we measure consciousness
directly, without asking the person to

217
00:15:13,620 --> 00:15:18,360
do anything? The closest thing we have
is the perturbational complexity index,

218
00:15:18,620 --> 00:15:24,090
developed by Marcello Massimini, Giulio
Tononi, and colleagues, and first published

219
00:15:24,140 --> 00:15:27,410
in 2013. It's nicknamed zap and zip.

220
00:15:27,460 --> 00:15:32,730
You zap the cortex with a pulse of transcranial
magnetic stimulation and record the

221
00:15:32,780 --> 00:15:36,580
brain's echo with E.E.G. Then you zip it:

222
00:15:36,760 --> 00:15:41,490
you compress the spatial and temporal
pattern of the response, using the same kind

223
00:15:41,540 --> 00:15:43,840
of algorithm that
compresses computer files.

224
00:15:44,020 --> 00:15:48,530
A conscious brain answers a perturbation
in a way that is both integrated, spreading

225
00:15:48,580 --> 00:15:53,140
widely, and differentiated, producing
a complex, hard-to-compress pattern.

226
00:15:53,460 --> 00:15:59,070
An unconscious brain responds either locally,
like a stone dropped in mud, or with a

227
00:15:59,120 --> 00:16:01,500
big stereotyped wave, like a bell ringing.

228
00:16:01,780 --> 00:16:04,580
Either way, the response
compresses easily.

229
00:16:04,840 --> 00:16:09,430
In the first study, values in dreamless
sleep and under anesthesia fell roughly

230
00:16:09,480 --> 00:16:13,930
between point one and point three, and
wakefulness ran from about point four to

231
00:16:13,980 --> 00:16:18,810
point seven. Vegetative patients were
low, minimally conscious patients

232
00:16:18,860 --> 00:16:23,770
intermediate, and locked-in patients, fully
conscious but paralyzed, in the waking

233
00:16:23,820 --> 00:16:29,590
range. In 2016, Silvia Casarotto and
colleagues calibrated a cutoff of point three

234
00:16:29,640 --> 00:16:34,935
one that separated conscious from unconscious
conditions in a benchmark group of one

235
00:16:34,985 --> 00:16:37,620
hundred and fifty people
without a single error.

236
00:16:38,080 --> 00:16:43,200
Applied to forty-three vegetative patients,
it flagged nine with high complexity,

237
00:16:43,440 --> 00:16:46,720
and six of those nine later
recovered signs of awareness.

238
00:16:47,000 --> 00:16:50,000
The most striking
calibration case is ketamine.

239
00:16:50,240 --> 00:16:54,440
At anesthetic doses it leaves
people completely unresponsive.

240
00:16:54,540 --> 00:16:59,450
But they often report vivid dreams
afterward, and under ketamine the complexity

241
00:16:59,500 --> 00:17:04,410
index stayed high, matching what people
later reported rather than how they looked

242
00:17:04,460 --> 00:17:08,740
from the outside. Notice where the
measure's credibility comes from, though.

243
00:17:08,940 --> 00:17:11,020
It's calibrated against report.

244
00:17:11,340 --> 00:17:16,330
Right. Every consciousness meter is ultimately
validated against people who can tell

245
00:17:16,380 --> 00:17:19,180
us, afterward, whether
they experienced anything.

246
00:17:19,560 --> 00:17:25,530
That's fine for humans. It becomes the deep
problem for machines in episode twelve.

247
00:17:25,580 --> 00:17:30,250
Some of the most powerful evidence comes
from switching consciousness off and back

248
00:17:30,300 --> 00:17:35,210
on. General anesthesia is the only
routine procedure that reliably interrupts

249
00:17:35,260 --> 00:17:37,260
experience and then returns it.

250
00:17:37,440 --> 00:17:40,280
But unresponsive is not
the same as unconscious.

251
00:17:40,880 --> 00:17:44,760
There's an old, simple trick called
the isolated forearm technique:

252
00:17:45,020 --> 00:17:50,110
before the muscle relaxant is given, a
tourniquet is inflated on one arm, so that

253
00:17:50,160 --> 00:17:55,090
hand can still move. In a 2017 study
across six hospitals, two hundred and sixty

254
00:17:55,140 --> 00:17:59,360
patients were asked, after
intubation, to squeeze with that hand.

255
00:17:59,900 --> 00:18:03,600
Twelve of them, about four and a half
percent, responded to the command.

256
00:18:04,120 --> 00:18:06,840
None of those questioned
afterward remembered anything.

257
00:18:07,080 --> 00:18:11,570
So for some patients, under some regimes,
there is connected awareness without

258
00:18:11,620 --> 00:18:16,340
memory, and the absence of a memory is
not proof that nothing was experienced.

259
00:18:17,000 --> 00:18:21,130
Anesthetics act through quite different
molecular mechanisms, yet the ones that

260
00:18:21,180 --> 00:18:25,790
abolish experience seem to share a breakdown
of integrated communication across the

261
00:18:25,840 --> 00:18:31,930
brain. In September 2026, a study led by
Andrea Luppi reported a common signature of

262
00:18:31,980 --> 00:18:34,040
anesthesia in six species:

263
00:18:34,090 --> 00:18:39,300
humans, macaques, marmosets, mice,
zebrafish, and nematode worms.

264
00:18:39,540 --> 00:18:41,920
The drugs and recording methods differed.

265
00:18:42,000 --> 00:18:47,190
But in every species, activity in different
parts of the nervous system became less

266
00:18:47,240 --> 00:18:50,800
coordinated, and its past
predicted its future less well.

267
00:18:51,120 --> 00:18:52,620
Deep structures matter too.

268
00:18:53,100 --> 00:18:58,290
In 2020, Michelle Redinbaugh and colleagues
found that stimulating a small region of

269
00:18:58,340 --> 00:19:03,090
the central thalamus could wake anesthetized
macaques while the anesthetic was still

270
00:19:03,140 --> 00:19:09,390
flowing. And in 2025, deep electrodes in
five human patients showed certain thalamic

271
00:19:09,440 --> 00:19:14,090
nuclei becoming active before the prefrontal
cortex on trials where a stimulus was

272
00:19:14,140 --> 00:19:17,120
consciously seen, as if
the thalamus were a gate.

273
00:19:17,460 --> 00:19:19,780
Sleep makes the same
point from another angle.

274
00:19:20,000 --> 00:19:21,580
You're not unconscious all night.

275
00:19:21,700 --> 00:19:24,040
You dream, and not only in REM sleep.

276
00:19:24,400 --> 00:19:29,810
In 2017, Francesca Siclari, Tononi, and
colleagues woke people repeatedly through

277
00:19:29,860 --> 00:19:33,450
the night and asked whether they
had been experiencing anything.

278
00:19:33,500 --> 00:19:38,950
Dreaming, in both REM and non-REM sleep,
went with less slow-wave activity and more

279
00:19:39,000 --> 00:19:41,760
fast activity in a posterior
region of the cortex.

280
00:19:42,160 --> 00:19:47,310
In a follow-up with seven sleepers, that
signal alone predicted in real time, during

281
00:19:47,360 --> 00:19:51,810
non-REM sleep, whether they would report
a dream, with about eighty-seven percent

282
00:19:51,860 --> 00:19:56,220
accuracy. We'll meet that posterior
hot zone again next episode.

283
00:19:56,460 --> 00:20:02,170
And in 2021, four labs reported two-way
communication with lucid dreamers, who

284
00:20:02,220 --> 00:20:06,870
solved simple arithmetic problems during
REM sleep and signaled their answers with

285
00:20:06,920 --> 00:20:10,580
eye movements. Psychedelics
push in yet another direction.

286
00:20:11,080 --> 00:20:16,270
Robin Carhart-Harris's entropic brain
hypothesis, and the model he developed with

287
00:20:16,320 --> 00:20:21,450
Karl Friston called REBUS, propose that
psychedelics relax the brain's high-level

288
00:20:21,500 --> 00:20:25,180
prior beliefs, letting bottom-up
signals flow more freely.

289
00:20:25,360 --> 00:20:30,090
That connects directly to the predictive
brain of episode five, and fits the finding

290
00:20:30,140 --> 00:20:34,580
that the diversity of brain signals, a
cousin of zap and zip's compression measure,

291
00:20:35,020 --> 00:20:38,120
rises under psilocybin, LSD, and ketamine.

292
00:20:38,380 --> 00:20:44,670
In 2024, a study in Nature by Joshua Siegel
and colleagues scanned people repeatedly

293
00:20:44,720 --> 00:20:47,760
before, during, and after
a dose of psilocybin.

294
00:20:47,900 --> 00:20:52,710
The drug desynchronized brain networks more
than three times as much as a stimulant

295
00:20:52,760 --> 00:20:57,970
control, most strongly in the default mode
network, the system most associated with

296
00:20:58,020 --> 00:21:02,510
self-referential thought. And reduced
coupling between the hippocampus and that

297
00:21:02,560 --> 00:21:04,040
network persisted for weeks.

298
00:21:04,960 --> 00:21:09,060
Then there's a source of evidence
that is very old and newly scientific:

299
00:21:09,460 --> 00:21:14,570
meditation. Contemplative traditions have
been mapping states of mind for more than

300
00:21:14,620 --> 00:21:20,510
two thousand years. The Mandukya Upanishad
distinguishes waking, dreaming, and

301
00:21:20,560 --> 00:21:24,420
dreamless sleep from a fourth state
it describes as pure awareness.

302
00:21:24,760 --> 00:21:29,430
Buddhist traditions describe meditative
absorptions called jhanas, and a state

303
00:21:29,480 --> 00:21:33,140
called nirodha samapatti,
the attainment of cessation.

304
00:21:33,480 --> 00:21:38,110
These are first-person claims, made by
people who have trained their attention for

305
00:21:38,160 --> 00:21:42,400
decades. On their own, they're
not data in the scientist's sense.

306
00:21:42,580 --> 00:21:45,760
But they generate hypotheses
that can now be tested.

307
00:21:46,060 --> 00:21:51,970
The philosopher Thomas Metzinger's 2024
book, The Elephant and the Blind, analyzes

308
00:21:52,020 --> 00:21:56,010
more than five hundred reports from
meditators of what he calls minimal phenomenal

309
00:21:56,060 --> 00:22:01,440
experience: awareness with little or no
content, and no sense of self or time.

310
00:22:01,700 --> 00:22:06,150
If that state exists, it's a precious
test case for any theory that ties

311
00:22:06,200 --> 00:22:09,240
consciousness to self-modeling
or to rich content.

312
00:22:09,500 --> 00:22:14,950
And in 2023, Ruben Laukkonen, Matthew
Sacchet, Heleen Slagter, and colleagues set

313
00:22:15,000 --> 00:22:17,280
out a research program
on meditative cessations:

314
00:22:17,740 --> 00:22:21,770
brief, apparently complete gaps in
consciousness that some advanced practitioners

315
00:22:21,820 --> 00:22:25,640
say they can induce at will, and much
longer ones in the traditional accounts.

316
00:22:25,960 --> 00:22:30,910
Early E.E.G. work has studied a single
advanced meditator intensively, across

317
00:22:30,960 --> 00:22:32,400
repeated cessation events.

318
00:22:32,740 --> 00:22:36,560
All of this is early, small, and
dependent on trained reports.

319
00:22:36,980 --> 00:22:39,360
Trained reports are still reports.

320
00:22:39,600 --> 00:22:43,390
Why trust a meditator more than
the shoppers of episode eight?

321
00:22:43,440 --> 00:22:48,670
On reports alone, you shouldn't, and every
caveat about introspection from episode

322
00:22:48,720 --> 00:22:53,970
eight applies. What training buys is
consistency, and the chance to pair reports

323
00:22:54,020 --> 00:22:59,310
with measurements. That's Varela's
neurophenomenology from last episode, finally

324
00:22:59,360 --> 00:23:03,710
getting data. And if consciousness can
be switched off by training as well as by

325
00:23:03,760 --> 00:23:08,220
drugs, that's an experimental
handle no anesthesiologist has.

326
00:23:08,440 --> 00:23:11,580
And any complete theory has
to account for the extremes:

327
00:23:11,980 --> 00:23:16,620
the cessations, the dreams under
ketamine, the awareness without content.

328
00:23:17,620 --> 00:23:22,360
The free-will experiments deserve care,
because they're so often summarized wrongly.

329
00:23:22,700 --> 00:23:27,820
In 1983, Benjamin Libet asked people to
flex their wrist whenever they felt like it,

330
00:23:27,960 --> 00:23:32,060
and to note, using a fast-moving
clock, when they first felt the urge.

331
00:23:32,320 --> 00:23:37,530
A slow buildup of electrical activity, the
readiness potential, began a few hundred

332
00:23:37,580 --> 00:23:40,930
milliseconds before the
reported moment of decision.

333
00:23:40,980 --> 00:23:41,980
The headline wrote itself:

334
00:23:42,280 --> 00:23:44,040
your brain decides before you do.

335
00:23:44,500 --> 00:23:46,480
Three later findings complicate it.

336
00:23:46,840 --> 00:23:52,490
In 2012, Aaron Schurger, Jacobo Sitt,
and Stanislas Dehaene showed that the

337
00:23:52,540 --> 00:23:56,670
readiness potential can be explained as
random neural noise drifting toward a

338
00:23:56,720 --> 00:23:59,380
threshold, with the movement
launched when it crosses.

339
00:24:00,080 --> 00:24:04,080
Averaged backward from the moment of
movement, that noise looks like a ramp.

340
00:24:04,240 --> 00:24:07,940
On this reading, the early signal
isn't a hidden decision at all.

341
00:24:08,460 --> 00:24:14,390
In 2008, Chun Siong Soon, John-Dylan
Haynes, and colleagues decoded which of two

342
00:24:14,440 --> 00:24:19,930
buttons someone would press from fMRI
activity several seconds in advance, but with

343
00:24:19,980 --> 00:24:23,670
accuracy only around sixty
percent, where chance is fifty.

344
00:24:23,720 --> 00:24:29,410
And in 2019, Uri Maoz and colleagues had
people decide which of two charities would

345
00:24:29,460 --> 00:24:31,920
receive a real thousand-dollar donation.

346
00:24:32,580 --> 00:24:37,350
The readiness potential was present when
the choice was arbitrary, and absent or

347
00:24:37,400 --> 00:24:39,782
much reduced when it was deliberate.

348
00:24:39,832 --> 00:24:43,618
So these experiments concern the
timing of arbitrary movements.

349
00:24:43,668 --> 00:24:46,603
They're interesting about
how actions get started.

350
00:24:46,653 --> 00:24:52,997
On their own, they fall far short of
refuting free will in the senses philosophers

351
00:24:53,047 --> 00:24:57,110
usually debate. Here's a question that
connects back to the first part of the

352
00:24:57,160 --> 00:25:02,670
series. If machines can do so much without
consciousness, how much can humans do

353
00:25:02,720 --> 00:25:05,180
without it? Less than the hype suggested.

354
00:25:05,580 --> 00:25:10,530
The famous 1957 claim that flashing the
words eat popcorn in a cinema boosted sales

355
00:25:10,580 --> 00:25:15,890
was a hoax. In 1962, its author admitted
it had been a gimmick, with too little data

356
00:25:15,940 --> 00:25:20,450
to mean anything. Unconscious thought
theory, which claimed that complex decisions

357
00:25:20,500 --> 00:25:23,800
go better when you're distracted,
has largely failed to replicate.

358
00:25:24,400 --> 00:25:28,790
Masked priming is real, but in careful
studies its effects are small and

359
00:25:28,840 --> 00:25:31,900
short-lived. The picture that
has survived is roughly this.

360
00:25:32,340 --> 00:25:37,530
Unconscious processing in humans is fast,
parallel, and specialized, and can extract

361
00:25:37,580 --> 00:25:42,610
some meaning from words. But flexible,
multi-step reasoning and acting on novel

362
00:25:42,660 --> 00:25:45,560
combinations seem to
depend on conscious access.

363
00:25:46,000 --> 00:25:50,990
Holding information over time mostly does
too, though some studies find faint traces

364
00:25:51,040 --> 00:25:53,600
of unseen items lasting a few seconds.

365
00:25:53,860 --> 00:25:59,240
Which is interesting, because in machines
that coupling apparently doesn't hold.

366
00:25:59,640 --> 00:26:04,390
Language models do multi-step reasoning,
and most researchers doubt they're

367
00:26:04,440 --> 00:26:08,480
conscious. That's one of the deepest
puzzles where our two topics meet.

368
00:26:08,800 --> 00:26:13,290
Either human brains couple flexible
reasoning with consciousness for contingent

369
00:26:13,340 --> 00:26:17,600
architectural reasons, in which case
machines show the coupling is optional.

370
00:26:17,860 --> 00:26:22,760
Or machines that reason flexibly have
something functionally like conscious access,

371
00:26:23,020 --> 00:26:25,220
whether or not anything is experienced.

372
00:26:25,620 --> 00:26:30,570
Episodes twelve and thirteen
take both readings seriously.

373
00:26:30,620 --> 00:26:36,950
Finally, animals. In 2020, Andreas Nieder
and colleagues trained two carrion crows

374
00:26:37,000 --> 00:26:39,580
to report whether they
had seen a faint stimulus.

375
00:26:40,260 --> 00:26:45,210
Neurons in a high-level region of the crow
forebrain tracked stimulus intensity at

376
00:26:45,260 --> 00:26:49,570
first. But during the delay before the
response, they tracked what the crow went on

377
00:26:49,620 --> 00:26:52,100
to report, including its false alarms.

378
00:26:52,460 --> 00:26:58,030
That's a neural correlate of subjective
report in a bird, whose brain, as we saw in

379
00:26:58,080 --> 00:27:02,940
episode two, arrived at cortex-like
circuits by a separate evolutionary route.

380
00:27:03,160 --> 00:27:08,450
In April 2024, a group of scientists
and philosophers launched the New York

381
00:27:08,500 --> 00:27:10,840
Declaration on Animal Consciousness.

382
00:27:11,000 --> 00:27:15,510
It states that there is strong scientific
support for attributing conscious

383
00:27:15,560 --> 00:27:17,440
experience to mammals and birds.

384
00:27:17,600 --> 00:27:21,880
It states that there is at least a realistic
possibility of it in all vertebrates,

385
00:27:22,020 --> 00:27:27,670
and in many invertebrates, including at
least cephalopods, decapod crustaceans, and

386
00:27:27,720 --> 00:27:32,230
insects. And it says that it is
irresponsible to ignore that possibility in

387
00:27:32,280 --> 00:27:34,100
decisions affecting those animals.

388
00:27:34,520 --> 00:27:38,980
About forty researchers signed at the
launch, and hundreds more have signed since.

389
00:27:39,180 --> 00:27:43,430
The method behind statements like that is
what the philosopher Jonathan Birch calls

390
00:27:43,480 --> 00:27:47,890
a theory-light approach. Rather than
commit to one theory, look for behavioral

391
00:27:47,940 --> 00:27:51,080
markers associated with
conscious experience in humans.

392
00:27:51,500 --> 00:27:55,120
Do animals trade off avoiding pain
against getting something they want?

393
00:27:55,340 --> 00:27:59,070
Do they tend their wounds, or learn
to avoid places where they were hurt?

394
00:27:59,120 --> 00:28:04,170
Hermit crabs given small shocks are more
willing to abandon a poor shell than a good

395
00:28:04,220 --> 00:28:08,680
one. In 2021, Robyn Crook gave
octopuses a painful injection.

396
00:28:09,020 --> 00:28:13,750
They tended the spot, later avoided the
chamber where it happened, and preferred one

397
00:28:13,800 --> 00:28:15,980
where a local anesthetic
had brought relief.

398
00:28:16,240 --> 00:28:19,640
The octopus from episode
two, it seems, can hurt.

399
00:28:19,800 --> 00:28:24,570
And the ball-rolling bumblebees from
episode two met the behavioral criteria for

400
00:28:24,620 --> 00:28:29,970
play. Episode fourteen returns to how
evidence of this kind changed the law in

401
00:28:30,020 --> 00:28:32,740
Britain. And none of that transfers to AI.

402
00:28:33,440 --> 00:28:35,040
That's the point to end on.

403
00:28:35,280 --> 00:28:40,070
Every marker in this episode was calibrated
in creatures that share our evolutionary

404
00:28:40,120 --> 00:28:46,510
history and our biology. In animals,
behavior and biology come bundled, so a

405
00:28:46,560 --> 00:28:49,520
behavioral marker is evidence
about the machinery underneath.

406
00:28:49,840 --> 00:28:54,830
A language model can produce any behavioral
marker you can name, trade-off talk and

407
00:28:54,880 --> 00:28:59,430
pain talk included, because it learned from
text written by creatures that have the

408
00:28:59,480 --> 00:29:04,790
machinery. For machines, theory-light
approaches break down, and we're forced back

409
00:29:04,840 --> 00:29:09,000
onto theory. Which is why the next
episode puts the theories on trial.
